# T7-004 -- plane migration under a changing layer set
#
# The backing store is re-split into two while the overlays above it stay
# where they are on screen. On vkms every overlay still has to move down a
# plane, because its planes expose no zpos property at all: stacking order is
# fixed by plane index, so a layer inserted below the overlays pushes every
# one of them along. That is forced, and the reference does it.
#
# Which makes this a migration test rather than a stability test. What it
# exercises is the ordering inside a single commit: every plane leaving an
# assignment must be cleared before any plane joining one is written. Get it
# backwards and the kernel sees two planes claiming the same stacking slot on
# the same CRTC in the same atomic request, rejects the whole thing, and the
# frame is dropped -- a migration that is perfectly valid as an end state
# looks invalid as a transition.
#
# It is worth having precisely because it is the case where a port can be
# correct about *what* it programs and wrong about the order, which no
# report or counter would show.

add bg  0 0 0 0 0
add ov1 1 64 64 256 256
add ov2 2 400 200 256 256

frame
frame

# One full-width backing store becomes two half-width ones. Both overlays
# shift up a plane as a result; neither changes what it shows.
del bg
add bgl 0 0 0 512 768
add bgr 0 512 0 512 768
frame
frame
